Version 6.8, November 21, 2025

No changes.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Version 6.7, January 27, 2025

8/12/25  [EDGcpfe/28388]
Unbounded allocation of memory on malformed IA-64 mangled name

Some malformed IA-64 mangled names could cause edg_decode to allocate a
large amount of memory, causing unspecified behavior.  Now fixed.


6/25/25  [EDGcpfe/28025]
Add supporting command-line options for writing named modules

The front end now supports the following modules-related options:

  --create_module_interface file
  Write an EDG IFC module file for a module interface unit to file.

  --create_module_internal_partition file
  Write an EDG IFC module file for a module implementation unit to file.

  --module_interface
  Write an EDG IFC module file for a module interface unit in the current
  working directory; the filename is derived from the parsed module
  declaration.

  --module_internal_partition
  Write an EDG IFC module file for a module implementation unit in the current
  working directory; the filename is derived from the parsed module
  declaration.

The following options have been removed: --ms_mod_interface,
--no_ms_mod_interface, --ms_internal_partition, --no_ms_internal_partition.
The new options are only required if creating an EDG IFC module file is
desired.


4/16/25  [EDGcpfe/28111]
Fix eccp overly greedy file collection

Previously, the command line argument parsing in eccp was overly greedy.  This
could result in an option like --ms_mod_file_map=foo=foo.cpp being treated as a
cpp file instead of a command line option.


4/15/25  [EDGcpfe/28103]
Thread safety for demangling

The code in decode.c has been modified to make it thread-safe when compiled
with MULTIPLE_THREAD_COMPILATION set to TRUE.  Notably, this change does not
introduce any multi-threaded demangling utility program.


1/27/25  [EDGcpfe/27888]
Replace uses of sprintf with snprintf

Uses of sprintf in the util directory have been replaced by safer calls to
snprintf.


1/2/25   [EDGcpfe/27800]
Multi-threaded POSIX front end daemon

A new multi-threaded daemon has been implemented and a corresponding new
utility program (cfe_daemon_client.c) has been added (see the Changes file in
the src directory for more information).


12/9/24  [EDGcpfe/27785]
Avoid stack overflow on non-mangled Cfront names with multiple underscores

Demangling strings in the Cfront ABI that contain "__" can cause the demangler
to assume that the strings are mangled identifiers when they aren't.  In the
case of a string like "x__update", the demangler had mistakenly tried to
demangle the identifier, resulting in a stack overflow.  That has now been
fixed.


12/9/24  [EDGcpfe/27786]
Modernize temporary file creation

eccp now uses mktemp to create temporary directories instead of the PID
(process identifier).


12/2/24  [EDGcpfe/27746]
Floating-point demangling changes

In the Cfront ABI, clang's __fp16 type is now recognized and demangled.

In the IA-64 ABI, the demangled names for the std::floatN_t types have been
changed to _FloatN (to better match the demangled names from GNU and clang).


11/11/24 [EDGcpfe/27715]
Fix eccp default argument processing

Previously, eccp would incorrectly prefer arguments specified in
EDG_CPFE_DEFAULT_OPTIONS over those specified explicitly on the command line.
In practice, this resulted in any option present in EDG_CPFE_DEFAULT_OPTIONS
being the only option eccp would accept.  Now fixed.


10/9/24  [EDGcpfe/27569]
Cfront ABI: read past end of buffer when demangling some invalid identifiers

In the Cfront ABI, decode_identifier had read past the end of the buffer
on some invalid identifiers (like "___it").  Now fixed.


9/16/24  [EDGcpfe/27560]
GNU/Clang compatibility: __GLIBCXX_BITSIZE_INT_N_0 and __GLIBCXX_TYPE_INT_N_0

The make_predef_macro_table tool has been updated to remove any predefined
macro settings for __GLIBCXX_BITSIZE_INT_N_0 and __GLIBCXX_TYPE_INT_N_0 as
those are now set dynamically in the front end.


1/30/24  [EDGcpfe/26984]
Cfront ABI: Demangling of _Float16, _Float32x, _Float64x types

The demangler had failed to demangle names that contain _Float16, _Float32x,
or _Float64x types and is now fixed.


