Version 4.1, August 6, 2009

7/27/09  [EDGcpfe/9554]
Demangling of lambdas and unnamed types

A change has been made to demangle the lambda and unnamed class/struct/union
and enum type encodings that are now emitted by the front end.  The Cfront
encodings are an EDG extension (though encoding-wise, they appear as any
user defined type -- except that they begin with "__U").  The IA-64 ABI
specification details the encoding of such types in C++0x.  Here is an
example that shows the original unnamed types, their encodings in both
ABIs as well as the decoded output in each case:

  int module_id = 0;
  struct { int i; } s;
      // IA-64:  _ZN25_INTERNAL_4_ex_c_afc2b591Ut_E
      //         [local to ex_c]::[unnamed type (instance 1)]
      // Cfront: __Q2_26__INTERNAL_4_ex_c_afc2b5915__Ut1
      //         [local to ex_c]::[unnamed type (instance 1)]
  struct A {
    enum { e = 1 } E;
      // IA-64:  _ZN1AUt_E
      //         A::[unnamed type (instance 1)]
      // Cfront: __Q2_1A5__Ut1
      //         A::[unnamed type (instance 1)]
    static int m;
  } a;
  int A::m = [](float x) { return (int)x; }(2.0);
      // IA-64:  _ZN1A1mMUlfE_E
      //         A::m::[lambda(float) (instance 1)]
      // Cfront: __Q3_1A1m8__Um1_Ff
      //         A::m::[lambda(float) (instance 1)]
  void f(int i = [](char a, char b) { return a - b; }('1', '0')) {}
      // IA-64:  _ZZ1fiEd_UlccE_
      //         f(int)::[default argument 1 (from end)]::
      //                                      [lambda(char, char) (instance 1)]
      // Cfront: __23__Ud1_1_FcT1__L0__f__Fi
      //         f(int)::[lambda(char, char) in default argument 1 (from end)
      //                                                          (instance 1)]


6/12/09  [EDGcpfe/9428]
Demangling of individuated entities

In C++0x, local and unnamed types can now be used as template type arguments,
meaning that local and unnamed types can now appear in the mangled names of
functions.  To ensure that these unnamed classes/enums as well as types defined
in static functions are differentiated from similar constructs in other
translation units, the type names are "individuated" by the front end.
Individuated entities are mangled "as if" they are part of a translation unit
specific namespace (this fabricated "namespace" incorporates the translation
unit's module id to provide a unique symbol).  Mangled names that are
individuated are now recognized during demangling and the individuated
namespace name is replaced with "[local to <filename>]" in the demangled
output.  This mangling scheme is used in both IA-64 and Cfront ABIs (and is an
EDG extension to the IA-64 ABI).

For example, these are mangled names for a function template that
takes an unnamed struct and an unnamed enum as template type arguments:

  _Z1fIN22_INTERNAL_3_x_c__Z2f1v4__C1ENS0_4__E2EEvT_T0_

is now demangled as (IA-64 ABI):

  f<[local to x_c]::__C1, [local to x_c]::__E2>(T1, T2)

and

  __CPR87__f__tm__67_Q2_23__INTERNAL_3_x_c_f1__Fv4__C1Q2_J13J4__E2__FZ1ZZ2Z_v

is demangled as (Cfront ABI):

 void f<T1, T2>(T1, T2) [with T1=[local to x_c]::__C1, T2=[local to x_c]::__E2]



3/13/09  [EDGcpfe/8056]
Demangling of rvalue references

The demangling of rvalue references (a C++0x feature) has been implemented.
 

3/13/09  [EDGcpfe/9611]
Change IA-64 mangled encoding of Microsoft explicitly overridden functions

The IA-64 ABI mangling of Microsoft explicitly overridden functions
used the local extension 'O' (see the Changes entry of 7/18/03).  The letter
'O' has since been designated to encode rvalue references in the IA-64 ABI.
To avoid a conflict, the letter 'Q' will now be used as a local extension to
the IA-64 ABI when encoding Microsoft explicitly overridden functions.
The Cfront ABI is unchanged.

1/19/09  [EDGcpfe/9439]
Extra space removed in demangler output on cv-qualifier on pointer type

The output of the Cfront ABI demangler has been tweaked slightly to
remove an extra space following a cv-qualifier on a pointer.  For
example,

  foo__tm__4_CPi__FZ1Z_i

is now demangled as

  int foo<T1>(T1) [with T1=int *const]
                                     ^extra space here eliminated


1/15/09  [EDGcpfe/7932, EDGcpfe/8999]
Detect array overflow in mk_errinfo

mk_errinfo has fixed size arrays that are used to build the table of
error messages and error tags.  We make sure that these arrays are large
enough for the error_msg.txt table that we provide, but customers could
cause them to overflow if they add their own messages.  mk_errinfo will
now issue an internal error if the tables need to be made larger.


12/11/08 [EDGcpfe/6851]
Generate include guard test in error message files

mk_errinfo now generates include guard tests in the files it produces.
These files are not actually included more than once by the front end,
but this was done in case customer changes should give rise to such a
condition.


