Version 4.0, December 5, 2008

11/3/08  [EDGcpfe/8945]
Demangling of typeid operator

A change has been made to demangle the typeid operator in a mangled
encoding for an expression.  In the Cfront ABI, the "ty" string indicates
the typeid operator; in the IA-64 ABI the strings "v17typeide" and "v16typeid"
are used (the former representation is used when typeid refers to an
expression, the latter a type).  This case comes up in Microsoft mode for
template parameters.


8/31/08  [EDGcpfe/9197]
Demangling of identifiers containing "$" in IA-64 ABI

The name demangler for the IA-64 ABI now handles identifier names
that contain the "$" character.

7/22/08  Demangling of GNU vector_size attribute

The demangler now handles mangled names that include the GNU
__atribute__((vector_size(N))), in both the Cfront and IA-64 ABIs.
Note that the IA-64 mangling matches gcc, which does not encode
the size in the name.

7/3/08   IA-64 ABI demangling of non-external names

The spec for the IA-64 ABI indicates that it should be possible to
call __cxa_demangle with a name that does not begin with "_Z"
and the name in that case should be considered to be a mangled type
name.  That is now handled by our version.  Note that this change
is not visible when edg_decode is run -- type names will still be
left unchanged when run through that filter.

6/10/08  getopt: handling of command line options beginning with "--"

Previously the local implementation of getopt treated any argument
that started with "--" as the argument "--" and returned EOF (-1).
Now getopt will return '?' when it finds an argument beginning with
the string "--" (that is not "--").  This change affects all utilities
in this directory that use getopt to parse command line arguments
(i.e., edg_decode, edg_munch, edg_prelink) on platforms where the local
version of getopt is used (i.e., when  (!__SYSV__ || __VMS__) is TRUE).
As a result, these utilities may issue an error when presented with
command line arguments that begin with "--" where the arguments were
silently ignored previously (the utilities differ in their handling of
these command line errors):

  $ edg_prelink --help
  unrecognized option: --help
  C++ prelinker: error: command line error

10/9/07  Prelinker: improved handling of parentheses and quoted strings in
         compiler invocations

The prelinker inserts escapes into the command it uses to recompile source
files.  It has been updated to better handle parentheses outside of quoted
strings and quoted strings with embedded quotes, escapes, and spaces.
For example, the following command can now be used to compile and link the
example below.  Formerly, this would fail when the compiler was invoked
from the prelinker.

  eccp -c -DSTR='"A\"BC"' -DCHR="'\''"  -c ex.c
  eccp ex.o

  extern "C" int printf(const char *, ...);
  template <class T> void f(T){}
  int main() {
    printf("%s %c\n", STR, CHR);
    f(1);
  }

As mentioned above, the change also allows spaces to be used in quoted strings,
but note that the eccp script does not handle arguments with embedded spaces.

